#%PAM-1.0
# W Linux — greetd PAM stack (security.md, point 6: gnome-keyring).
#
# Mirrors the greetd package default (system-local-login, no password phase); the
# only W additions are the two `pam_gnome_keyring` lines, which unlock the user's
# login keyring (Secret Service) with the login password at sign-in:
#   auth    — captures the password (must come AFTER system-local-login's pam_unix)
#   session — `auto_start` launches gnome-keyring-daemon and unlocks the keyring,
#             exporting GNOME_KEYRING_CONTROL into the launched user session.
# Strictly `optional`: a keyring failure never blocks login.
#
# Caveat: auto-unlock needs the password. Fingerprint-only login leaves the keyring
# locked (PAM never sees a password) — expected, not a bug.
# SSH is NOT handled here: gcr-ssh-agent (separate, swappable layer) owns the agent.

auth       required     pam_securetty.so
auth       requisite    pam_nologin.so
auth       include      system-local-login
auth       optional     pam_gnome_keyring.so
account    include      system-local-login
session    include      system-local-login
session    optional     pam_gnome_keyring.so auto_start
